BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261104T180000Z
DTEND:20261104T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261104T000000Z
SUMMARY:Daytime Book Club: Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil
DESCRIPTION:Come join the Daytime Book Club as we discuss this month's 
 book! New members are always welcome (we love meeting new people!). 
 Registration is appreciated but not required.\n\nThis month we will discuss 
 Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil by John Berendt. The description 
 for the book is below:\n"Shots rang out in Savannah's grandest mansion in 
 the misty\,early morning hours of May 2\, 1981. Was it murder or 
 self-defense? For nearly a decade\, the shooting and its aftermath 
 reverberated throughout this hauntingly beautiful city of moss-hung oaks 
 and shaded squares. John Berendt's sharply observed\, suspenseful\, and 
 witty narrative reads like a thoroughly engrossing novel\, and yet it is a 
 work of nonfiction. Berendt skillfully interweaves a hugely entertaining 
 first-person account of life in this isolated remnant of the Old South with 
 the unpredictable twists and turns of a landmark murder case.\n\nIt is a 
 spellbinding story peopled by a gallery of remarkable characters: the 
 well-bred society ladies of the Married Woman's Card Club\; the turbulent 
 young redneck gigolo\; the hapless recluse who owns a bottle of poison so 
 powerful it could kill every man\, woman\, and child in Savannah\; the 
 aging and profane Southern belle who is the "soul of pampered 
 self-absorption"\; the uproariously funny black drag queen\; the acerbic 
 and arrogant antiques dealer\; the sweet-talking\, piano-playing con 
 artist\; young blacks dancing the minuet at the black debutante ball\; and 
 Minerva\, the voodoo priestess who works her magic in the graveyard at 
 midnight. These and other Savannahians act as a Greek chorus\, with Berendt 
 revealing the alliances\, hostilities\, and intrigues that thrive in a town 
 where everyone knows everyone else."\n\nWe look forward to seeing you all 
 soon!\n\n\nInterested in attending a future Daytime Book Club meeting? Take 
 a look at our reading list below:\n\nMay 2026: To the Lighthouse by 
 Virginia Woolf\n\nJune 2026: The Night Watchman by Louise Erdrich\n\nJuly 
 2026: A Marriage at Sea by Sophie Elmhirst\n\nAugust 2026: My Friends by 
 Fredrik Backman\n\nSeptember 2026: Wild Dark Shore by Charlotte 
 McConaghy\n\nOctober 2026: The House on Mango Street by Sandra 
 Cisneros\n\nNovember 2026: Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil by John 
 Berendt\n\nDecember 2026: Orbital by Samantha Harvey\n\nJanuary 2027: What 
 We Can Know by Ian McEwan\n\nFebruary 2027: Song of Solomon by Toni 
 Morrison\n\nMarch 2027: Proud Shoes by Pauli Murray\n\nApril 2027: Isola by 
 Allegra Goodman
LOCATION:East Regional - Children's Programming Room\, East Regional Library (211 Lick Creek Lane)
ORGANIZER;CN="Mara Plantholt":MAILTO:mplantholt@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Book Club
UID:LibCal-14288-16576159
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16576159
END:VEVENT
